      {
        "narration": "Her car sat abandoned above a quarry, lights still on. December 4th, 1926, Newlands Corner, Surrey. Clothes across the seats.",
        "visual_query": "1920s english countryside chalk hillside",
        "visual_fallback": "chalk hillside"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The driver was Agatha Christie, already a bestselling novelist. She had walked out of her Sunningdale home the night before, after a row about a divorce.",
        "visual_query": "1920s vintage car country lane",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage car"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Over a thousand police searched. Fifteen thousand volunteers. Aeroplanes too, the first time aircraft hunted a missing civilian.",
        "visual_query": "english countryside search 1920s aerial",
        "visual_fallback": "english fields"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Arthur Conan Doyle handed one of her gloves to a spirit medium. Eleven days passed. The country decided she was dead.",
        "visual_query": "harrogate victorian spa hotel exterior",
        "visual_fallback": "spa hotel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "She was in Harrogate, 184 miles north, at a spa hotel, registered as Mrs Teresa Neele, the surname of the woman her husband loved. Two doctors found a genuine loss of memory. Her autobiography never mentions the eleven days.",
        "visual_query": "1920s hotel lobby interior",
        "visual_fallback": "hotel lobby"
